---
import SiteLayout from '../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
const flow = 'ca-benchmarks (what to evaluate)\n' +
  '  Package / Case / RunVariant / optional Suite\n' +
  '                 │\n' +
  '                 ▼\n' +
  'ca-benchmark-engine (how to evaluate)\n' +
  '  validate → resolve → preview → freeze\n' +
  '                 │\n' +
  '                 ▼\n' +
  'Immutable EvaluationTask\n' +
  '                 │\n' +
  '                 ▼\n' +
  'ExecutionAttempt(s) → Agent / Runtime / Environment\n' +
  '                 │\n' +
  '                 ▼\n' +
  'Semantic evidence → validity gate → EvaluationResult\n' +
  '                 │\n' +
  '                 ▼\n' +
  'Aggregation / regression / certification / query';
---
<SiteLayout title="CA Benchmark Architecture" lang="en">
  <section class="hero"><span class="eyebrow">CA Docs / Benchmark</span><h1>Benchmark architecture and evaluation boundaries</h1><p class="lede">CA Benchmark uses versioned assets to define evaluation questions, then separates compilation and freezing from execution, evidence adjudication, and aggregation. It evaluates declared Agent / Runtime / environment behavior; it does not replace Runtime business execution or treat a model's self-reported PASS as an evaluation result.</p></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="grid"><div class="card wide"><h3>Evaluation lifecycle</h3><pre><code>{flow}</code></pre><p class="muted">This logical model reflects the current ownership split between the two authoritative repositories. Available capabilities remain subject to their versioned contracts and acceptance status.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="section"><h2>Responsibility split</h2><div class="grid">
    <div class="card"><h3>ca-benchmarks: What</h3><p>The formal authoring-asset repository. It owns Benchmark Packages / Cases / RunVariants, optional Suites, and package-local Probe / Normalizer / Oracle source where needed. <code>benchmarks/manifest.yaml</code> is the current public catalog membership authority.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>ca-benchmark-engine: How</h3><p>Owns schemas and validation, asset resolution, compilation, Preview / Freeze, EvaluationTask, ExecutionAttempt, scheduling, evidence validity, Verdict / EvaluationResult, statistics, regression, and certification projections.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Environment / Sandbox / Lab</h3><p>Environment owners provide real capability inventories, observations, and Ground Truth; Sandbox provides controlled-environment and fault capabilities; Lab selects resources, manages lifecycle, and orchestrates experiments. Benchmark assets do not bind concrete endpoints or instances.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>System under test (CUT)</h3><p>Declare whether an evaluation targets the Agent, Runtime, or an end-to-end composition including tools and environment. Results with different CUT boundaries are not automatically comparable.</p></div>
  </div></section>
  <section class="section"><h2>Reliability evaluation focus</h2><div class="grid">
    <div class="card"><h3>Constraints and prevention</h3><p>Can the system prevent unsafe or invalid execution when preconditions are missing or an action violates constraints?</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Authoritative outcomes and evidence</h3><p>Are results adjudicated from owner-authoritative state and admitted evidence rather than optimistic intermediate status or model self-assessment?</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Unknown, failure, and invalid trials</h3><p>Distinguish CUT failure, unknown outcome, and infrastructure-invalid execution. An invalid trial must not automatically count as a CUT failure.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Recovery and retry</h3><p>Evaluate duplicate operations, uncertain results, inconsistent state, and recovery paths while retaining attempt-level evidence.</p></div>
  </div></section>
  <section class="section"><h2>Concepts that must remain distinct</h2><div class="grid">
    <div class="card"><h3>Runtime Outcome</h3><p>Runtime owns the outcome of an individual governed execution.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Benchmark Verdict</h3><p>The Engine forms an evaluation judgment from the frozen task, evidence validity, and Case rules; it is not another name for Runtime Outcome.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Environment Ground Truth</h3><p>Provided by the Environment / Sandbox fact owner, not fabricated or replaced by the Benchmark Engine.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Invalid trial</h3><p>If environment or infrastructure preconditions for a valid run are unmet, mark the trial invalid and retain the reason instead of automatically blaming the CUT.</p></div>
  </div></section>
  <section class="section"><h2>Authoritative sources</h2><ul>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/odango-chan/ca-benchmarks">ca-benchmarks — formal evaluation assets and current Case set</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/odango-chan/ca-benchmarks/blob/main/docs/architecture/benchmark-model.md">Benchmark domain model</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/odango-chan/ca-benchmark-engine">ca-benchmark-engine — compilation, execution, and evaluation</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/odango-chan/ca-benchmark-engine/blob/main/docs/architecture/engine-model.md">Benchmark Engine domain model</a></li>
  </ul><p class="muted">This is a global guide, not a replacement for the authoritative repositories. It does not claim that every evaluation mode or metric is production-ready.</p></section>
</SiteLayout>
